Singleton ensures that only one object of a class is ever created in your entire program, and it gives you a way to access that one object.
Why use Singleton?
To share a single resource, like a printer, database connection, or config file.
To save memory and avoid creating many copies of the same object.
To make sure only one point of control exists (centralized).

creating a singalton object.
Step 1: Create a private static variable to hold the one and only instance
Step 2: Make the constructor private so no one can create objects from outside
Step 3: Create a public method to get the single instance

| Term          | Meaning                                           |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| **Instance**  | A real object created from a class                |
| **Static**    | Belongs to the class, not to objects              |
| **Private**   | Can't be accessed directly from outside the class |
| **Singleton** | Only one instance exists, shared everywhere       |
